    {
      "id": "a1-u01-d01-t01",
      "before": "a1-u01-d01-e01",
      "title": { "en": "How Polish is read" },
      "body": { "en": "Polish is read the way it is written: a letter almost always keeps the same sound. The vowels a, e, i, o and u are short and pure. The stress falls on the second-to-last syllable: do-BRA-noc. Polish w sounds like English v, j like the y in \"yes\", and c like the ts in \"cats\"." }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1-u01-d01-t02",
      "before": "a1-u01-d01-e04",
      "title": { "en": "Hello and goodbye" },
      "body": { "en": "Dzień dobry is the polite hello, used all day until the evening. Cześć is for friends and family, and means both hi and bye. Letters with marks, such as ś, ć, ń and ę, have their own sounds. This Unit teaches them a few at a time, so for now just listen and copy. Tap the speaker to hear the Polish again, or Slow to hear it slowly." }
    }
  ],
